# Keep `version` and `date-released` in step with the tag being published.
# Until v0.6.0-rc1 this file described the Python application that was removed
# in the rewrite: it advertised structure prediction and inverse folding,
# neither of which OpenDNA does any more.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use OpenDNA, please cite it as below."
authors:
  - family-names: "Kleckner"
    given-names: "Zachary"
    email: "zkleckner@dyber.org"
title: "OpenDNA: protein property prediction that runs on your own machine"
version: 0.6.0-rc1
date-released: "2026-11-16"
url: "https://github.com/dyber-pqc/OpenDNA"
repository-code: "https://github.com/dyber-pqc/OpenDNA"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - protein engineering
  - protein property prediction
  - intrinsic disorder
  - solvent accessibility
  - protein stability
  - protein solubility
  - protein language model
  - ESM-2
  - bioinformatics
  - open source
  - reproducible research
abstract: >
  OpenDNA predicts per-residue and whole-protein properties from sequence
  alone, on ordinary hardware and without a network connection. Four trained
  heads read frozen ESM-2 embeddings: intrinsic disorder, relative solvent
  accessibility, the stability change of a point mutation, and solubility.
  Each ships a model card generated from its own measured training report, and
  each prediction carries the model build, the device and the card's
  limitations in the output file, with a run manifest recording the digests of
  what went in and what came out. A head that is not installed is reported as
  unavailable rather than estimated some other way. It is distributed as a
  command-line tool for batch work and a desktop application with a structure
  viewer.
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Lin"
        given-names: "Zeming"
    title: "Evolutionary-scale prediction of atomic-level protein structure"
    year: 2023
    journal: "Science"
    volume: 379
    pages: "1123-1130"
    doi: "10.1126/science.ade2574"
    notes: >
      ESM-2, the protein language model every head reads its embeddings from.
